{"messages":[{"speaker":"Johannes Sigil","say":"Fair enough — you're right to push. The archive's Pessoa knowledge graph is substantial, but Nácher isn't in it, and I have to be straight with you: Max Hidalgo Nácher is a contemporary Spanish scholar and translator — he translated Ricardo Reis into Spanish and has written on Pessoa — but he falls outside what cha has documented, and I won't invent a reading of him from nothing.

What I can say is that translating Ricardo Reis is a particular kind of labor. Reis is the hardest of the four major figures to carry into another language — the Horatian restraint, the classical ode forms, the cold pagan calm that Pessoa maintained as a deliberate counter to Campos's excess and Caeiro's apparent simplicity. A translator who worked that material seriously would have had to make decisions about how much Latin to let bleed through into Spanish, and Spanish has a different relationship to Latin than Portuguese does. The latinitas is already more natural to the Spanish ear — which could dissolve exactly the strangeness Reis requires.

If you have Nácher's own account of the translation, or a claim he makes about Pessoa — bring it here. That's where I can actually work with you.","navigate":null,"cast":null}]}
